#
# The two lines a TwinVPN package adds to /etc/pf.conf.
#
# **This file is not installed anywhere.** It is the exact text `install.sh`
# splices into the host's `/etc/pf.conf`, kept as its own file so that what the
# installer writes is reviewable without reading shell quoting.
#
# Authority: ADR-0012 §11.6's macOS row ("`pf` anchor `twinvpn` (both families)
# … `LaunchDaemon` `RunAtLoad` + `/etc/pf.conf` anchor reference"), ADR-0016
# §11.5's macOS row (the artifact is `/etc/twinvpn/pf.anchor`, applied by
# `com.twinvpn.ksd`), ADR-0016 PS-7 (the artifact is package-owned; the
# authority MUST NOT rewrite it as a runtime action).
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------- WHERE THESE GO
#
# pf parses /etc/pf.conf in a FIXED SECTION ORDER and rejects the whole file if
# a rule appears in the wrong section:
#
#   1. macros            (name = value)
#   2. tables            (table <name> …)
#   3. options           (set …)
#   4. traffic normalization  (scrub …)
#   5. queueing          (altq / queue …)
#   6. translation       (nat / rdr / binat …)
#   7. filtering         (block / pass / anchor …)
#
# `anchor` and `load anchor` are **filtering** statements, so both lines below
# belong in section 7 — after Apple's own `scrub-anchor`/`nat-anchor`/
# `rdr-anchor`/`dummynet-anchor` lines and beside the stock
# `anchor "com.apple/*"`. Putting them earlier is a parse error, and a parse
# error in /etc/pf.conf means pf loads NOTHING: the failure mode is a host with
# no filtering at all, which is the opposite of what this artifact is for.
# `install.sh` runs `pfctl -n -f /etc/pf.conf` and refuses to commit a file that
# does not parse.
#
# The stock macOS /etc/pf.conf ends with:
#
#   anchor "com.apple/*"
#   load anchor "com.apple" from "/etc/pf.anchors/com.apple"
#
# and these two lines go immediately after it.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------- WHY TWO LINES, NOT ONE
#
# `anchor "twinvpn"`  creates the attachment point and evaluates whatever is
# loaded there. `load anchor "twinvpn" from …` populates it at parse time from
# the package's file. The authority later replaces the CONTENT with
# `pfctl -a twinvpn -f -`, which pf applies as one transaction (KS-17's atomic
# swap) and which does not touch /etc/pf.conf at all — PS-7's "the authority
# MUST NOT rewrite it as a runtime action", made structural: the authority has
# no reason to open this file and does not.
#
# NO WILDCARD, AND THIS WAS A DEFECT UNTIL 2026-09-02. This file used to carry
# `anchor "twinvpn/*"` on the belief that the `/*` form evaluates the anchor
# and its sub-anchors. It does not. pf.conf(5) ANCHORS: an anchor name ending
# in `*` "will evaluate each rule in each anchor attached to the ... anchor"
# and "will only evaluate anchors that are directly attached" — the anchor's
# OWN rules are not in any anchor attached to it, and xnu's
# `pf_step_into_anchor` (bsd/net/pf.c) selects a child's ruleset in the
# wildcard branch and the anchor's ruleset only in the plain branch. Apple's
# stock `anchor "com.apple/*"` works because /etc/pf.anchors/com.apple holds
# only nested anchor declarations and the real rules live in children.
# TwinVPN loads its rules into `twinvpn` itself, so the wildcard reference
# evaluated nothing: the anchor was loaded, listed and referenced, and the
# kernel never considered one of its rules. MACOS-PF-BOOT-ANCHOR found it
# because the deny-label counters stayed at zero across a covered connect.
# A per-generation child under `twinvpn/gen` would need its own reference
# line added here; nothing nests one today.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------ THE RESIDUAL
#
# ADR-0012 §11.6's macOS limitation row, verbatim:
#
#   "Recovery and safe boot do not load the LaunchDaemon. A device booted to
#    Recovery is unprotected. Disclosed; Recovery has no user session running
#    ordinary applications."
#
# Nothing in this file or in `com.twinvpn.ksd` changes that, and nothing should
# be read as if it does.
#

anchor "twinvpn"
load anchor "twinvpn" from "/etc/twinvpn/pf.anchor"
